m at  the minimum  of the van diemer curve where the  efficiency uh  is uh is the best and you can see that  nitrogen  actually has a lower height equivalent  of theoretical plate  and so this has the the best resolution  potentially the best resolution  and the the best efficiency  unfortunately that happens at a lower  speed you can see the linear velocity  where this occurs  is somewhere around 10 centimeters a  second  so what happens is people don't really  consider nitrogen because they don't  want to  wait for two or three times longer to  get their results  however most gcs use constant flow mode  and so with changes in flow due to  changes in temperature  those are not observed as they were in  the past before  programmable epcs  okay so to demonstrate converting a  helium method  to nitrogen or to hydrogen  using the simplest process possible i'm  looking here at a test case  this is again another astm method it's  the 6584.  it's used to measure contaminants in  biodiesel specifically mono  dye and triglycerides and also  glycerol so i'm showing the instrument  conditions here  so the astm method uses helium carrier  gas  at three mils a minute and the  uh to run in the constant pressure mode  at about 7.63  psi on the inlet and this just shows the  region where the peaks of interest are  alluding  now the first step when changing from  helium to  either hydrogen or nitrogen is of course  to plumb the carrier gas to the  instrument  then we have to tell the instrument that  we have made  a carrier gas change so to do this you  go into the configuration  screen for your particular gc go to the  inlet where you change the carrier gas  and select the appropriate gas  so in this case we have a coupon column  inlet that we're going to change  and we go from helium to hydrogen or  nitrogen  after making that change go over and  look at the column section and look at  the flow so as i said  this method uses three mls a minute of  flow but we also want to look at