ects of our  methods that we have to take into  account  in general methods require less  optimization  when going to hydrogen carrier gas if  the analytes are durable  hydrogen is a reactive gas it's a  reducing gas  so those compounds that won't react with  hydrogen are best suited  also compounds that are at higher  concentrations  are also more suitable when using  hydrogen carrier gas  particularly with a mass spectrometer in  general  hygiene carrier gas will offer a little  less  sensitivity than helium with mass  spectrometers  also in general analytes that are used  with split injections  are more suitable with hydrogen carrier  gas particularly if those are  more reactive and that's because with  split injections  the residence time of the injection  is lower inside the inlet and so there's  less of a chance of reaction with  hydrogen carrier gas  with split injections also  compounds that have been derivatized to  improve their  gas chromatographic performance  will also be better suited for hydrogen  and that's because the functional groups  that are reactive  that cause poor chromatographic  performance  have been derivatized to make them  better chromatographic  compounds and those active sites  have been masked by the derivatization  compound  so they won't react with the hydrogen  methods that will generally require more  optimization  are of course the opposite any fragile  compounds will have first have to be  evaluated with hydrogen  to make sure that they aren't changed or  lost  in the presence of hydrogen also if  you're looking at the absolute lowest  trace level concentrations  you'll have to validate your method with  hydrogen first that you can  achieve those because as i said in  general  hydrogen is a less sensitive carrier gas  particularly for mass spec  operations before you complete your  change from helium to hydrogen you also  have to allow the necessary time  to update any sops and method validation  with the change in the carrier gas  now some considerations when switching  the hydrogen  you have to be aware of the flow  limitations due to the mass spec pumping  capacity  so oftentimes hydrogen is run at higher  flows  because you get better  performance chromatographic performance  so if you're going to use those higher  flows you have to make sure that your  mass spec  is has an adequate pumping system like a  turbo pump rather than a diffusion pump  to accommodate that higher flow  also there can be changes in peak  illusion order with the carrier gas  so you will have to revalidate those  peaks making sure they're identified  properly  and then you'll also have to consider  the column sample capacity  because that can change with the use of  a different carrier gas  as i said hydrogen is not an inert gas  it's a reducing gas  so it can react with the compounds that  you're trying to measure  so you also need to consider the full  inert flow path and we generally  recommend  to use inert columns we have ultra inert  columns and alternate flow paths  that may be more appropriate with  hydrogen carrier gas  you also want to use the lowest  temperatures possible  this particularly true of your inlets  and for instance  transfer lines with the mass spec again  higher temperatures will cause hydrogen  to be more reactive  and most importantly is you want to  avoid some solvents  particularly using split less injections  and hydrogen carrier gas  so those would be methylene chloride  carbon disulfide  that's because they have a long  resonance time and a high concentration  in the inlet at the time of injection  and they will react with the hydrogen  and they'll cause the formation of  hydrochloric acid and also sulfuric acid  and that will cause definite maintenance  issues with the inlet  and poor performance of a system  now before switching particularly those  you have older mass specs  the 5977 series is okay those are  completely proven  for hydrogen use some of the older 5975  systems ho