 to see significant hailing  if you  don't do that conditioning and in the  right hand chromatogram  you can see that in this case there's  some drugs of abuse that show extreme  tailing and particularly the cocaine  peak  so this is going to require some initial  startup to try to minimize these effects  now for any gc or gcms system there is  going to be a initial setup  and a cooperation time when switching  from helium to hydrogen carrier gas  and for gcms i'm listing some of those  steps here  so after you plumb in your hydrogen  carrier gas to your gc  you're going to allow the system to set  up overnight  to clean using the hydrogen carrier gas  so after doing the plumbing you'll pump  down the system  you'll set your source temperature to  the maximum temperature  for your particular source depending on  the mass spec that you're using  at the same time you're going to reduce  your electron multiplier voltage down to  800 volts  then you're going to run the system  overnight with the filaments turned on  and what this will do is this will  ionize the hydrogen coming into the  source  and this will allow the system to be  cleaned so you're going to run this  overnight  and then the next day come in and  do a matrix check to be sure that  everything looks okay  if it looks okay then you're ready to go  so this shows i uh the checkout  after doing the overnight cleanup with  hydrogen  and so we're going to compare the  results with the helium run  so after conversion from helium to  hydrogen we can see the chromatography  here  there is still somewhat some tailing  that can be expected depending on the  compounds  other compounds may not tail at all so  you can see  for instance that large peak at about  seven and a half minutes  there's not very much tailing at all and  in fact the response in hydrogen seems  to be better than in helium  however some of the other peaks are  showing some tailing and some reduced  response  and that's just the nature of using  hydrogen with a gcms system  so we have their conversion from helium  to hydrogen  what can you expect in your analytical  results on a gcms system  first the sensitivity can potentially be  reduced  on the order of two to five times  reduction in sensitivity  again hydrogen is a larger molecule and  it's easier  to ionize it than helium and so that is  going to reduce  some of the sensitivity in the source  some trace  reactive compounds may be lost  and you may not see them at all and then  also you can see that there could be  changes  in this actual mass spectra or the  spectral fidelity  so there could be some changes in the  observed ion ratios for some compounds  and there also could be missing or extra  ions and this will actually affect  uh your library searching so in this  example we show lindane  and you can see that in the upper  chromatogram with helium  uh the lower mass ions are somewhat  reduced  however if we look in the hydrogen  carrier gas  we're getting larger response for those  lower molecular weight ions and so this  will  affect the search so you can see in the  helium carrier gas  the search quality was 925  but with hydrogen it's been reduced  somewhat to 859  and then in the table on the right this  is a number of different  uh pesticides and you can see that the  difference in  this library match between helium and  hydrogen  does show somewhat of a difference and  you'll have to be prepared for that when  evaluating your result  now before getting into the details of  converting methods from helium to  hydrogen  i'm first going to have to discuss some  hydrogen safety features that are built  into the instrument  and then also some best practice  practices  for making the use of hydrogen as safe  as possible  we can provide for all of our agilent  gcs  a letter from agilent safety engineer  that describes the built-in features for  our instruments for using hydrogen in  the safest possible manner  these are available for our latest  instruments the 8890  8860 and also the intubo gc  in this slide we show some of the safety  features that are built into our  instruments that are described in the  letters  first of all we have a safety shutdown  so whenever  the gas pressure set points are not met  so this would be because of a leak or a  broken column  the valve and the heater are shut off so  the valve turns off  the hydrogen delivered from the epcs to  the instruments  and then the heaters are also  simultaneously shut off and this  prevents  any possible explosions that could take  place with buildup of hydrogen in the  instrument  we also have built into all of our epcs  that are hydrogen compatible a flow  limiting fret  so if the valve fails in any way  and stays in the open position these  frits  minimize the amount of hydrogen that can  be delivered  through the epc and into the instrument  also in our on off sequence for the oven  before the heaters are turned on when  turning the instrument on  the fan will come on and will purge the  oven  with multiple volumes of exchange of the  internal  gas chromatography oven to remove any  collected hydrogen  only after this has been run for a few  minutes  then the heaters will be turned on and  then finally we also do some  vigorous explosion tests for all our  instruments  where we put into the inside of the  instrument a lower explosive limit of  hydrogen we ignite that hydrogen  and then we make sure that if there is  an internal explosion  that no pieces come off of the  instrument  to be projectiles that could hurt or  injure  any laboratory personnel that are near  the instrument at the time of an  explosion  as a second level of safety we also  offer an optional  hydrogen sensor module this is  goes into the back of the gc it's  available for  a number of our more modern instruments  so show a  picture of it here it's a board that  goes into the back of the gc  and on the board is a long probe that  comes through  the back of the instrument and into the  actual calm oven  and this is a sniffer port so this  actually draws  the atmospheric gas inside the oven into  the  module or the hydrogen sensor module and  measures the amount of hydrogen that can  be detected  so the way this works is we install that  in the instrument  and then there's also comes with a  lecture bottle  of some hydrogen that's  at about 1 hydrogen in an inert gas  this can be used to calibrate the sensor  and you can also set up how often the  sensor becomes calibrated so this is  available  for uh our most modern instruments our  current instruments the 8890 and the  8860  and then also for some of the older  instruments  the 7090a plus so if you have 7890as  you could get this if you upgrade it to  an a plus  then all the 7090bs are also compatible  with the hydrogen sensor module  sources of hydrogen can be varied uh we  prefer or what we recommend are hydrogen  generators  we show the parker hydrogen generator  here  this generates hydrogen by filling it  with water  and it hydrolyzes the water to convert  it  to oxygen and hydrogen and then we'll  deliver that  to your gas chromatograph and you can  get these in various sizes to  accommodate different  maximum flows of hydrogen that required  for  multiple instruments for  hydrogen cylinder delivery we recommend  a clean gas filter there are third-party  devices that can be added to the  plumbing  outside of the gc that will shut off the  cylinders if there is a loss of pressure  or if a leak is detected we don't sell  those but  the environmental health and safety  officers  and for most companies know where to  acquire these  for many laboratories hygiene is  delivered from a common source  throughout the building  and so of course the local  building maintenance people  environmental health and safety people  typically do a routine hydrogen safety  check  now when plumbing your gc 2 whatever  hydrogen source you're using  we're recommending to use really good  quality stainless steel tubing  we also say don't use older tubing these  can be contaminated  just by sitting around the laboratory  and hydrogen is known as a scrubbing gas  and what you'll see is a high background  so use  new stainless steel tubing or tubing  that's been  thermally cleaned we don't recommend  using  copper tubing especially old copper  tubing copper does become brittle over  time  there's also some indication that  hydrogen flowing through copper tubing  accelerates the brittleness and so this  can cause  breaks in the tubing and leaks and  delivery of hydrogen where you don't  want it  for your split vents you want to make  sure that your split vents are connected  to an exhaust because you are going to  be putting  sometimes high flows of hydrogen out  into the laboratory atmosphere so you  don't  want to let those set off any alarms and  then also when you're plumbing up a new  system or making any changes to your  instrument  you want to be sure that you check for  leaks we don't really recommend  snoop especially because  it is a surfactant  and if that gets into your flow path it  can cause contamination problems  so we really do recommend using a leak  checker  we have one they are more expensive but  they work extremely well  and they're very good for detecting  hydrogen leaks  now when converting methods from helium  to an alternative carrier gas  these are just some bullet points here  we want to make sure we  follow all the safety protocols and  recommendations for your facility  so you want to start when converting  from helium to an alternative you want  to start with an existing helium method  it's easier than trying to create an  entirely new method with a new carrier  gas  then we provide a software tool called  method translation software  to help you make that transition  to the alternative carrier gas from an  existing helium method  so method translation software assumes  that you're using the same  column both the column phase and the