ntain  our pollution order and retention times  so we can see the effect of a  translation of 5769  so in the upper chromatogram i have my  helium carrier gas this is a  calibration standard and if we look at  the retention times we can see that the  run goes from with peaks from about six  minutes out to  just under 30 minutes then after i've  used the method translation software  to go from helium to hydrogen you can  see that there's  almost no change in retention time a  very minimal change in the retention  times  and then also uh the resolution  well it's about the same and there's no  changing of the peak aleutian order  and so what this allows us to do is uh  have minimal changes to the method  because we're measuring all these  compounds we want to make sure that our  integration parameters and our  peak identification and quantification  parameters  remain the same and so this will help  with setting up the method  for going from helium to hydrogen  carrier gas  then this is an example of after the  conversion running  a sample so this is an actual gasoline  sample  that was used in a proficiency testing  program  so the upper chromatogram shows the  helium carrier  result and the bottom shows the hygiene  carrier result  you can see that pretty much they look  the same  the hydrogen looks a little busier  but if we look at the results  and that's shown in this slide uh the  results between the helium  and the hydrogen carrier gas are nearly  identical  so for our benzene tylene result  0.23 volume percent of benzene  0.3 for helium  and pretty much the same for hydrogen  carrier gas for the total aromatics  we have about 19.65 with helium  and 19.68 for hydrogen and those match  well with the consensus values  that other laboratories have determined  for this particular sample  now of course besides helium and  hydrogen  nitrogen is another carrier gas that can  be used  and serves as a potentially good  alternative  to helium if we're trying to save helium  a number of methods that are used in  chemical and energy or what we used to  call  hydrocarbon processing industry are well  suited to nitrogen  things like simulated distillation where  absolute resolution isn't required  a nitrogen may be a very good choice as  an alternative  to helium some of the advantages of  nitrogen  it's readily available and it's less  expensive  so you can find it in good  chromatographic grades pretty much  anywhere in the world  aside from using pressurized gas  cylinders there's no other safety  concerns with nitrogen  as there would be with hydrogen because  it's it's not a reactive or  explosive gas uh it's really  very suitable for simple routine  analysis  where you have more than enough  resolution  and then nitrogen is also suitable for  some 2d gc techniques  these would be where we're using two  different columns to optimize resolution  so we're using the column to give us the  best resolution  and so the nitrogen may be a good  carrier guess for this  these are typically for pack columns  using valves where we're using multiple  columns and valves  to tweak a separation however for  the more sophisticated 2d check  2d technique techniques like gcgc  nitrogen is not really a suitable  carrier gas  some potential issues for nitrogen there  are reduced chromatographic  resolution at higher flows at the flows  that we're typically used to using with  helium  so you won't get the same resolution and  again it can't really be used with gcms  and some other gc detectors because of  loss of sensitivity  so when trying to convert from helium to  an alternative carrier guess  we're going to take an easy approach as  i described earlier we're going to take  an existing helium method and we can use  some of the firmware  in the gc system to make this conversion  easy even without using method  translation software  so the goal here is to change the  carrier gas  while keeping all the other method  conditions exactly the same so we're