irely lost  and so in this case if we were  interested in maintaining that  resolution  then nitrogen would not be the best  carrier gas of choice  as an alternative hydrogen would be  now if we look at what we're actually  doing with this analysis  is we're reporting as an aggregate uh  the monoglycerides so in this case  uh the resolution of that pair  is not really important is because we we  integrate them and report them together  so in this example we could use  nitrogen as a carrier gas because even  though we've lost resolution  it doesn't really affect the overall  results because we would have integrated  those together  anyway and we're not really looking for  a complete resolution  again this has to be a judgment that you  make  is the carrier guest delivering for you  the resolution that's required by your  analysis  so those results are shown here so no  matter what carrier guess we use  we're getting the same result for all of  our different classes of compounds with  the sample  so in this case i would say either  hydrogen or nitrogen would be an  appropriate carrier gas to use  for this particular method because this  doesn't really affect the overall  results on the chromatography  required by this method  so in summary i've shown that  we can easily migrate your gc method  conditions  from a helium carrier gas so  for methods where resolution is  absolutely critical  hydrogen will offer the best alternative  and also for gcms methods hydrogen will  be the best alternative  for many gc applications nitrogen  can be used it offers a cheap and easy  alternative without the safety  worries that can sometimes accompany  hydrogen  most existing helium methods do have too  much resolution  so a change in nitrogen even at the same  analysis speeds  uh generally won't affect that if again  you have sufficient resolution  nitrogen can be used without changing  any of the existing gc conditions  as long as you keep the hold up time the  same  and not try to 